                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h2]Bendy and the Ink Machine, a disappointing masterpiece[/h2]\n\nI remember back in 2017 when Bendy first came out. It was the golden age of mascot horror, and the amount of hype it got was insane. Bendy stood up there with Freddy as one of the icons of mascot horror. Every Let's Player was talking about it, but I never got to play it myself. Here and there, I learned about it through snippets and retellings, but never experienced the game as a whole. And I have to say, I was intrigued. The story sounded fascinating. An animator called Joey Drew who believes that with enough willpower anything can come to life, his company being a reference to Walt Disney and the American golden age of animation as a whole, how it all turned to ruin, and how the being he once created sought revenge for being abandoned. It sounded like a masterpiece. Thus, I was always excited to finally play it, especially looking at the relatively strong fandom that's still alive today.\n\nAfter playing all the chapters and going for 100% completion, I have to say I'm disappointed, and not just a bit. Yes, quirks with the engine and buggy gameplay are clear negatives, but for a small indie game they're more than excusable. What isn't, though, is that the player gets insulted. The game is stupidly boring. It's just objective based mission after objective based mission with no real depth or excitement, while at other moments the game spells solutions out so clearly that I feel like the main audience might be 12 year olds. For example, during the music puzzle in Chapter 2, the character says more than once while standing in the music room that there must be something here he has to do. Just why? Or take the incredibly monotonous Chapter 3, where we have to do fetch quests for Alice multiple times, with all of them basically being the same. It's just so incredibly boring that I had to force myself to keep playing.\n\nBut what about the story? The concept sounded amazing, and yes, the story holds up, but sadly gets majorly underdeveloped and stagnates rather quickly. Once the concept is established, it gets built upon here and there, but receives no major development. Yes, we get introduced to Alice and so on, but nothing substantial. All of that makes the later chapters feel like the game was being stretched far beyond the ideas that originally made it interesting.\n\nBut the one thing I have to commend almost without flaw is the graphics and design. The game looks amazing. It has a unique retro art style tapping into the golden age of animation with a bit of Borderlands charm. The same goes for the enemies. Some are boring, like the simple ink blobs, but the Projectionist, the Butcher Gang, Boris, and Alice are all such amazing designs. But sadly, Bendy himself is a disaster in design. His normal form is underwhelming and a bit boring, which can be excused. After all, it's not his final form. But his final form is even more simplistic and boring. He just transforms into a big blob of ink with monster like proportions. As the flagship villain hyped up for four chapters, there should be a magnum opus of design here, but there just isn't.\n\nThe same goes for his boss fight. You expect glitz and glamour, going out with a bang, but it's just objectives again and a simple charge against four supports. It's a boss fight seen so often in gaming that I can't stand it. And that's where I see the main issue with Bendy. As mentioned, it starts with a bang and goes out with a whimper. For something discussed as one of the GOATs of mascot horror, I expected something revolutionary, not just a mid tier, somewhat enjoyable horror game.\n\nAnd that's what it is. An okayish experience. I would still call it a definite must play for anyone deeply interested in horror games and their history who wants to understand how the genre developed. But as an actual recommendation based purely on the quality of the experience, it just isn't all that. Especially for something that, after FNAF, is now even in discussions of getting a movie. Just why?\n\nMaybe Dark Revival carries the franchise. To be fair, I haven't played that one yet. But this is its flagship product. It shouldn't serve mediocrity. If you're a casual horror fan, play something else. There are so many better experiences out there.",
